## Styling

Tailwind CSS, via the [standalone CLI](https://tailwindcss.com/blog/standalone-cli)
(no Node.js/npm) — `pytailwindcss` (a `pyproject.toml` dependency) downloads
and manages the platform binary the first time you run it. The source of
truth is `src/{{ package_name }}/static/css/input.css`; the compiled
`static/css/style.css` `<link>`ed from `templates/base.html` is a build
artifact, git-ignored, and **must be rebuilt after editing `input.css` or
adding new Tailwind classes to a template**:

```
# One-off build:
uv run tailwindcss -i src/{{ package_name }}/static/css/input.css -o src/{{ package_name }}/static/css/style.css

# Or watch and rebuild on every change, while developing:
uv run tailwindcss -i src/{{ package_name }}/static/css/input.css -o src/{{ package_name }}/static/css/style.css --watch
```

Custom design tokens (like the accent color) live in `input.css`'s
`@theme` block — Tailwind v4's CSS-first config, no `tailwind.config.js`
needed.{% if docker %} `docker build` runs the one-off build automatically
as part of the image, so the container always ships current CSS.{% endif %}
